.TH "CSSUNPARSEDVALUE" "3JS" "August 15, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSUnparsedValue \- CSSUnparsedValue
.SH SYNOPSIS
\f[B]Note:\f[R] This feature is available in Web Workers.
.PP
The \f[B]\f[CB]CSSUnparsedValue\f[B]\f[R] interface of the CSS Typed
Object Model API represents a property value that can\(cqt be parsed
into a more specific type \(em typically the value of a custom property.
.PP
The object is an iterable that may contain string fragments and variable
references.
.PP
The items can be accessed and set by index
(\f[CR]unparsedValue[0]\f[R]), and as an iterable it can be used with a
\f[CR]for...of\f[R] loop or the spread syntax.
.SH CONSTRUCTOR
.TP
\f[B]CSSUnparsedValue()\f[R]
Creates a new \f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R] object.
.SH INSTANCE PROPERTIES
.TP
\f[B]CSSUnparsedValue.length\f[R] \f[I](read\-only)\f[R]
Returns the number of items in the object.
.SH INSTANCE METHODS
\f[I]Also inherits methods from its parent interface,
\f[CI]CSSStyleValue\f[I].\f[R]
.TP
\f[B]CSSUnparsedValue.entries()\f[R]
Returns a new \f[I]array iterator\f[R] that yields
\f[CR][index, value]\f[R] pairs for each item in the object.
.TP
\f[B]CSSUnparsedValue.forEach()\f[R]
Executes a provided function once for each item in the object.
.TP
\f[B]CSSUnparsedValue.keys()\f[R]
Returns a new \f[I]array iterator\f[R] that yields the index of each
item in the object.
.TP
\f[B]CSSUnparsedValue.values()\f[R]
Returns a new \f[I]array iterator\f[R] that yields each item in the
object.
.SH DESCRIPTION
A \f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R] instance is an iterable of items, where
each item is either a string representing a fragment of CSS text that
wasn\(cqt further parsed, or a \f[CR]CSSVariableReferenceValue\f[R]
object representing a \f[CR]var()\f[R] function in the value (if any
were specified).
.PP
For example, given the custom property:
.IP
.EX
\-\-foo\f[BI]:\f[R] 10px var\f[BI](\f[R]\-\-bar, blue\f[BI])\f[R];
.EE
.PP
The \f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R] for \f[CR]\-\-foo\f[R] has two items:
.IP \(bu 2
\f[CR]\(dq10px \(dq\f[R]: a plain string fragment.
.IP \(bu 2
A \f[CR]CSSVariableReferenceValue\f[R]: representing
\f[CR]var(\-\-bar, blue)\f[R], with its own \f[CR]variable\f[R]
(\f[CR]\(dq\-\-bar\(dq\f[R]) and \f[CR]fallback\f[R] (a nested
\f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R] for \f[CR]blue\f[R]).
.SS Parsing a \f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R] further
\f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R] preserves the raw, unparsed text of a value:
it doesn\(cqt interpret that text as a length, a color, or any other
more specific type.
.PP
If you know what kind of value the text represents, you can parse it
further using the static \f[CR]parse()\f[R] method of the type you
expect, such as \f[CR]CSSNumericValue.parse()\f[R] for a numeric value
with a unit, or the more general \f[CR]CSSStyleValue.parse()\f[R], which
additionally requires you to specify the target CSS property name.
.PP
These \f[CR]parse()\f[R] methods take a CSS text string, not a
\f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R] object.
However since \f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R] inherits the
\f[CR]CSSStyleValue.toString()\f[R] stringifier, it can be passed
directly wherever a string is expected.
Parsing throws a \f[CR]SyntaxError\f[R] if the text doesn\(cqt match the
syntax expected by the type you\(cqre parsing it as.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Creating, reading, and updating a \f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R]
This example creates a \f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R], then reads its items
via \f[CR]length\f[R], indexed access, and iteration, and finally
updates one of the items by assigning to its index.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] value = \f[B]new\f[R] CSSUnparsedValue([\(dq1em\(dq, \(dq#445566\(dq, \(dq\-45px\(dq]);

console.log(value.length); \f[I]// 3\f[R]
console.log(value[0]); \f[I]// \(dq1em\(dq\f[R]

\f[B]for\f[R] (\f[B]const\f[R] fragment \f[B]of\f[R] value) {
  console.log(fragment);
}
\f[I]// \(dq1em\(dq\f[R]
\f[I]// \(dq#445566\(dq\f[R]
\f[I]// \(dq\-45px\(dq\f[R]

value[0] = \(dq2em\(dq;
console.log(value[0]); \f[I]// \(dq2em\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.SS Reading and parsing a custom property\(cqs value
A \f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R] is what you get back when you read the
value of a custom property from a \f[CR]StylePropertyMapReadOnly\f[R],
since the browser cannot know in advance what kind of value the property
holds.
This example reads the \f[CR]\-\-unit\f[R] custom property from an
element\(cqs computed style map, then parses it into a
\f[CR]CSSUnitValue\f[R] using \f[CR]CSSNumericValue.parse()\f[R].
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]button\f[R] id=\(dqbtn\(dq>Styled button</\f[B]button\f[R]>
.EE
.IP
.EX
btn {
  \-\-unit: 1.2rem;
  \f[B]padding\f[R]: var(\-\-unit);
}
.EE
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] styleMap = document.getElementById(\(dqbtn\(dq).computedStyleMap();
\f[B]const\f[R] unit = styleMap.get(\(dq\-\-unit\(dq);

console.log(unit); \f[I]// CSSUnparsedValue {0: \(dq 1.2rem\(dq, length: 1}\f[R]
console.log(unit[0]); \f[I]// \(dq 1.2rem\(dq\f[R]

\f[B]const\f[R] parsedUnit = CSSNumericValue.parse(unit);
console.log(parsedUnit.value); \f[I]// 1.2\f[R]
console.log(parsedUnit.unit); \f[I]// \(dqrem\(dq\f[R]
.EE
.PP
See the \f[CR]CSSUnparsedValue\f[R] section of the \f[I]CSS Typed OM
guide\f[R] for more detail on this example.
